    "knowledge_point": "Finding a Point That Lies on a Transformed Curve",
    "problem": "The point $(a,b)$ lies on the curve $y={2}^{x}.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y={2}^{x-2}+3?$",
    "worked_solution": "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y={2}^{x-2}+3,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y={2}^{x}.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $2$ units to the right and get the graph of $y={2}^{x-2}.$ After this transformation, the point $(a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a+2,b).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $3$ units up and obtain the graph of $y={2}^{x-2}+3.$ After this transformation, the point $(a+2,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a+2,b+3).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(a,b)$ lies on the graph of $y={2}^{x}$, we have that ${2}^{a}=b.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y={2}^{x-2}+3.$ Substituting $x=a+2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={2}^{a+2-2}+3 \\\\  & ={2}^{a}+3 \\\\  & =b+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(a+2,b+3)$ must lie on $y={2}^{x-2}+3.$",
